# Nested Flows

A Flow can call another Flow as a step target or as a Choice target. The child
Flow has its own input schema, nodes, and output schema. The parent maps data
into the child and receives the child's output as one node result.

Run the code cells from top to bottom.

## Install Jido Action

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido_action, "~> 3.0.0-rc.1"}])
```

## Define The Child Action

```elixir
defmodule NestedFlows.Describe do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "nested_flows_describe",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{value: Zoi.integer(), trace_id: Zoi.string()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.object(%{value: Zoi.integer(), trace_id: Zoi.string()})

  @impl true
  def run(params, _context), do: {:ok, params}
end
```

## Define A Child Flow

The child receives only the input map that its parent maps to it. The runtime
context is also available inside the child, so the child can read caller
metadata without putting it into the child input map.

```elixir
defmodule NestedFlows.Child do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "nested_flows_child",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{value: Zoi.integer()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.object(%{value: Zoi.integer(), trace_id: Zoi.string()})

  flow do
    step "describe",
      action: NestedFlows.Describe,
      params: %{
        value: input(:value),
        trace_id: context(:trace_id)
      }
  end
end
```

## Use A Child Flow As A Step

The parent maps `value` into the child. The child output becomes the result of
the parent `:child` node.

```elixir
defmodule NestedFlows.ParentStep do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "nested_flows_parent_step",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{value: Zoi.integer()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    step "child",
      action: NestedFlows.Child,
      params: %{value: input(:value)}

    output %{child: result("child")}
  end
end

{:ok, %{child: %{value: 4, trace_id: "trace-1"}}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(
    NestedFlows.ParentStep,
    %{value: 4},
    %{trace_id: "trace-1"}
  )
```

The child input schema validates the mapped `%{value: 4}` input. The child
output schema validates the result before the parent receives it.

## The Parent Sees One Atomic Node

Nested execution is atomic from the parent execution's view. `start/4` exposes
the parent node name. `step/2` executes the complete child Flow and returns the
child output in one `NodeResult`.

```elixir
{:ok, execution} =
  Jido.Exec.start(NestedFlows.ParentStep, %{value: 4}, %{trace_id: "trace-1"})

["child"] = Jido.Exec.ready(execution)

{:ok,
 %Jido.Exec.NodeResult{
   node: "child",
   status: :ok,
   output: %{value: 4, trace_id: "trace-1"}
 }, execution} = Jido.Exec.step(execution, "child")

:succeeded = Jido.Exec.status(execution)
{:ok, %{child: %{value: 4, trace_id: "trace-1"}}} = Jido.Exec.result(execution)
```

There is no public step-into API. A parent step does not expose the child
nodes. Run the child Flow separately when you need to inspect or step through
the child graph.

## Use A Child Flow As A Choice Target

A Choice can select a child Flow in the same way that it selects an Action.
The selected child must satisfy the input and output contracts declared by the
child Flow.

```elixir
defmodule NestedFlows.Echo do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "nested_flows_echo",
    schema: Zoi.map(),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  @impl true
  def run(params, _context), do: {:ok, params}
end

defmodule NestedFlows.ParentChoice do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "nested_flows_parent_choice",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{route: Zoi.atom(), value: Zoi.integer()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    choice "route" do
      option "child",
        condition: input(:route) == :child,
        action: NestedFlows.Child,
        params: %{value: input(:value)}

      otherwise action: NestedFlows.Echo,
                params: %{route: :fallback, value: input(:value)}
    end
  end
end

{:ok, %{value: 5, trace_id: "trace-2"}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(
    NestedFlows.ParentChoice,
    %{route: :child, value: 5},
    %{trace_id: "trace-2"}
  )

{:ok, %{route: :fallback, value: 5}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(
    NestedFlows.ParentChoice,
    %{route: :other, value: 5},
    %{trace_id: "trace-2"}
  )
```

The Choice remains one parent node. The selected child Flow is not represented
as a separate ready node in the parent execution.

## Parent Execution Options Do Not Propagate

`async: true` and `max_concurrency` control scheduling of independent nodes in
the execution being run. They do not propagate into a nested child Flow. A
child Flow runs with its own execution options (the defaults when called as a
nested target).

```elixir
{:ok, %{child: %{value: 4, trace_id: "trace-async"}}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(
    NestedFlows.ParentStep,
    %{value: 4},
    %{trace_id: "trace-async"},
    async: true,
    max_concurrency: 2
  )
```

These options do not create a child step or enable a child Flow's independent
nodes to run concurrently. Pass options when running the child directly if
you need to control that child execution.

